pr_body:
The only ipc_service backend currently available is the RPMsg multi-instance backend. This backend has two major limitations: (1) its DT support it is quite limited and (2) it assumes that there are multiple instances (with different priorities) connecting two (and only two) cores. The user can then dinamically allocates endpoints to communicate with the remote core and the endpoint-instance matching is done on a priority-basis.

This approach works fine when in the platform there are only two cores (domains) but this fails short in a real multi-core platform.

The main need in a real multi-core platform is the possibility to have multiple instances connecting different cores and the possibility to register endpoints on a specific instance. This patch is introducing a new backend, called RPMsg multi-core backend, that is trying to fill this void.

When using this backend the developer must take care of describing each instance in the DT (using properties for SHMEM, IPMs, priority, etc...) and the backend will register at boot each instance. At run-time the user can register an endpoint on a specific instance, specifying the name of the instance to use.

DT example where core `1` is connected to core `2` and `3` using two instances:

```
mailbox12: mailbox@44000000 {
        reg = <0x44000000 0x8000>;
        label = "IPM_0";
};

mailbox13: mailbox@45000000 {
        reg = <0x45000000 0x8000>;
        label = "IPM_1";
};

sram12: memory@42000000 {
        compatible = "mmio-sram";
        reg = <0x42000000 0x8000>;
};

sram13: memory@43000000 {
        compatible = "mmio-sram";
        reg = <0x43000000 0x8000>;
};

ipc_core12 {
        compatible = "ipc-rpmsg-mc";
        target = "core2";
        priority = <10>;
        ipc_shm = <&sram12>;
        ipm_rx = <&mailbox12>;
        ipm_tx = <&mailbox12>;
        ipm_tx_id = <10>;
        status = "okay";
};

ipc_core13 {
        compatible = "ipc-rpmsg-mc";
        target = "core3";
        priority = <10>;
        ipc_shm = <&sram13>;
        ipm_rx = <&mailbox13>;
        ipm_tx = <&mailbox13>;
        ipm_tx_id = <11>;
        status = "okay";
};

```
At run-time the user can register the endpoint on one of the two instances specifying `core2` or `core3` in the `struct ipc_ept_cfg` when using `ipc_service_register_endpoint()`.

Fixes #37130

