Ok, let's try to put down some preliminary info about this.

The minimum set of information we need to define an IPC instance between two CPUs is:
- The shared memory region
- The IPM device

This is the information that we should gather from the DT. The target CPU is a piece of information that is not needed per se (because the shared memory region is enough to define the two CPUs involved in the communication) but it will probably be needed for the API implementation.

In the current codebase this information is carried by the DT using some `chosen` entries:
```
/ {
	chosen {
		/*
		 * shared memory reserved for the inter-processor communication
		 */
		zephyr,ipc_shm = &sramx1;
		zephyr,ipc = &mailbox0;
	};

	sramx1:memory@4000000{
		compatible = "mmio-sram";
		reg = <0x4000000 0x8000>;
	};
};
```
This of course doesn't scale at all with the number of CPUs involved, so this must be reworked.

I have two proposals on how to extend this to support multi-core IPC:

**Proposal 1**
The shared memory regions are defined as `reserved-memory` nodes (see #35460) and each IPC instance is defined using a different node. So for example this is the DT for the core 1 when two IPC instances are defined to communicate with cores 2 and 3.

```
/ {
	reserved-memory {
		compatible = "reserved-memory";
		...

		ipc_smh_core12: ipc_smh@42000000 {
			reg = <0x42000000 0x1000>;
		};

		ipc_smh_core13: ipc_smh@43000000 {
			reg = <0x43000000 0x1000>;
		};
	};

	mailbox_core12: mailbox@4008b000 {
		compatible = "mailbox";
		reg = <0x4008b000 0xEC>;
	};

	mailbox_core13: mailbox@4008c000 {
		compatible = "mailbox";
		reg = <0x4008c000 0xEC>;
	};

	ipc_core12 {
		compatible = "ipc";
		ipc_shm = <&ipc_smh_core12>;
		ipc = <&mailbox_core12>;
	};

	ipc_core13 {
		compatible = "ipc";
		ipc_shm = <&ipc_smh_core13>;
		ipc = <&mailbox_core13>;
	};
};
```
**Proposal 2**
This is similar to the proposal 1 but all the different IPC instances are gathered together under a single node. The IPC multi-instance driver will be in charge to create and instance for each node child.
```
/ {
	reserved-memory {
		compatible = "reserved-memory";
		...

		ipc_smh_core12: ipc_smh@42000000 {
			reg = <0x42000000 0x1000>;
		};

		ipc_smh_core13: ipc_smh@43000000 {
			reg = <0x43000000 0x1000>;
		};
	};

	mailbox_core12: mailbox@4008b000 {
		compatible = "mailbox";
		reg = <0x4008b000 0xEC>;
	};

	mailbox_core13: mailbox@4008c000 {
		compatible = "mailbox";
		reg = <0x4008c000 0xEC>;
	};

	ipc_core1 {
		compatible = "ipc_multi_instance";

		core2 {
			ipc_shm = <&ipc_smh_core12>;
			ipc = <&mailbox_core12>;
		};

		core3 {
			ipc_shm = <&ipc_smh_core13>;
			ipc = <&mailbox_core13>;
		};
	};
};
```

@hubertmis @maje-emb  I wast starting to work on this but I think we need to clarify and discuss a bit the terminology because I think I'm confused about some choices done by the current code. Please, also refers to https://www.openampproject.org/docs/whitepapers/Introduction_to_OpenAMPlib_v1.1a.pdf to match my reasoning.

So, this is my interpretation of the things:

----
An **RPMsg instance** is a communication channel between two and only two cores. This is characterized and identified by a shared memory region, an IPM mechanism (or device) and a name. This information is found in the DT, so for example we can define an RPMsg instance with something like this:
```
ipc_core12 {
    compatible = "ipc-service,rpmsg-mi";
    target = "core2";
    ipc_shm = <&ipc_smh_core12>;
    ipc = <&mailbox_core12>;
};
```
Now, in theory we could have multiple RPMsg instances between the same two cores but I don't see how that could be useful and the current driver doesn't support that, so I'm going to assume that we have one and only one instance for each core-to-core communication (i.e. one instance for `A<->B`, one for `A<->C`, etc...).

The **RPMsg instances** are allocated at boot time reading all the info from the DT.

----

An **RPMsg endpoint** provides a software / logical connections on top of the channel / instance.

For example if we have an instance / channel between `A` and `B`, and we have two tasks running on `A` that need to communicate for different reasons with `B`, the two tasks can allocate two different endpoints in the same channel / instance. The endpoints allocation is done at run-time by the task: for example the task can request a new endpoint for the target `core2` (see the previous DT snippet).

So basically an endpoint is always associated to one and only one instance, it's what the task is using to communicate with a remote task / processor.

----

Assuming we agree on the previous points, I don't fully understand why IPC must be treated as a subsystem and not a driver / API. An instance / channel is basically a device (with its own DT entry) that is providing a service (in the form of an endpoint) to a user. So my question is: why cannot simply introduce an IPC API backed by an IPC driver to which the user can submit requests such as: allocating an endpoint, sending data through that endpoint, etc...

I envision an API such as:
```
__subsystem struct ipc_driver_api {
    allocate_endpoint();
    remove_endpoint();
    send_through_endpoint();
};
```

Each backend is implementing this API on a different `compatible` and an application can use this API as usual. This approach is way easier and cleaner than introducing a new subsystem, backends, etc...

Am I missing something obvious in my reasoning?
